123
00:08:41,490 --> 00:08:46,050
So Marco, there was a phrase that I heard twice in today's dialogue that I think we should talk about.

124
00:08:46,430 --> 00:08:50,310
The verb is to tackle, so we heard to tackle a question.

125
00:08:50,710 --> 00:09:01,270
Right, so when Mr. Ford says, I'll tackle that question a little bit later on, he's talking about I'll answer the question or I'll talk about that question later on.

126
00:09:01,690 --> 00:09:13,150
Right, but in this word to tackle, there's something kind of physical about it because in football we say that when someone tries to hit the ball from someone else, it's a slide tackle.

127
00:09:13,970 --> 00:09:21,730
And so to tackle a question means to really jump into it and try and solve it and it's you know maybe not an easy question.

128
00:09:22,440 --> 00:09:23,960
So I'm going to tackle that later.

129
00:09:23,960 --> 00:09:27,360
I'm going to tackle a challenge.

130
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:30,000
It's more than just to solve a problem or to answer a question.

131
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:33,600
So maybe you can also tackle a project, a difficult project.

132
00:09:33,600 --> 00:09:39,080
That's right, so you know what, I'm going to give us two weeks to tackle this project because it's a very difficult one.

133
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:40,560
Alright, very good.

134
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:57,920
And well actually when he was getting some questions from the audience members specifically because he was kind of being interrupted, he was very polite about it and to kind of defer the question he said, you know what, that's a fair point but I'll tackle that question later on.

135
00:09:57,920 --> 00:10:06,540
Okay, and so this is a really great phrase if you want to flatter someone, make them feel good before you tell them that you're not going to answer their question.

136
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:14,220
Or maybe you disagree with someone so I could say, you know what Marco, chocolate cake is the only cake in the entire world that people should eat.

137
00:10:14,620 --> 00:10:19,710
Okay, and I could say something like that's a fair point but in my opinion vanilla cake is better.

138
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:22,000
Alright, so see what Marco did?

139
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:22,950
He made me feel good.

140
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:34,880
He said, you know, fair point, that's a good idea but I disagree or but in the case of today's dialogue but we'll talk about that later.

141
00:10:34,880 --> 00:10:48,260
Right, and actually he did another smart thing in the way that he used his language skills here when he was interrupted again he said, hey, you know what, I'd be happy to answer your questions at a later time or in the Q&A session.

142
00:10:48,680 --> 00:10:50,780
Okay, so this phrase, I'd be happy to.

143
00:10:51,160 --> 00:10:57,700
I'd be happy to talk about that at a later time but right now I'm trying to explain something else.

144
00:10:58,560 --> 00:11:08,670
Exactly, so these are two really, really key phrases for trying to in a nice way change the topic or change the conversation.

145
00:11:09,030 --> 00:11:19,530
So yeah, I guess you can say, yeah, I'd be happy to talk about that later on or I'll tackle that question in a little bit or that's a fair point but we'll talk about it later on.

